First, suppose that no students are enrolled in more than one of the classes.  In this case, the number of students not in any of the classes and taking at least some class would be: 
$360 - (\text{number of students in mathematics} + \text{number of students in science} + \text{number of students in history})$ 

Now suppose that each student enrolled in a class also takes with them from $0$ to as many other classes they are enrolled in.  Let's call the variable $x$ the number of students enrolled in the history.  By the First Count, this is equal to:
$360-(\text{number of students in math}+\text{number of students in science}+x)$.
By the Second Count, this is equal to:
$360 - 15 - x$.

So setting the two equations equal to each other for $x$, we get:
$\begin{split}
360- (\text{number of students in math} + \text{number of students} &\text{ in science}+x) \\
= 360-15-x
\end{split}$ 

So:
$\text{number of students in math}+\text{number of students} \text{ in science}=15$.

By looking at a Venn Diagram and using logical reasoning for the variable from the first count, we get:
$\text{number of students who are only in math}+ (30-25) + 75 +75 + x = \text{number of students in math}$.
It follows that the latent variable for this equation equals $x$, the number of students in this room who only take history.  So $x$ is determined to be 0.
Thus, it follows that the number of students enrolled in calculus equals the number of students who take only math, the $30-25$ students taking the  chemistry and physics double classes but not the calculus double class, plus the 75 students who take chemistry and the 75 students do take physics.  
Using the principle of inclusion exclusive: 
$375-(375-30)=\boxed{30}
