Arguably one option to solve this problem is to first check whether the numbers 117 and 119 are prime.
These numbers are not prime and it is very likely you saw that from the factors.
Now a good way forward would be to find the prime factors of $117$ and $119$ and then multiply them.
First, we look at the number $117$. The prime factorization of $117$ is $3\cdot 3 \cdot 13$.
Next, we examine $119$. The prime factorization of $119$ is $7 \cdot 17$.
By multiplying the factors, we get:
\begin{align\*}
117 \cdot 119 &amp;= (3 \cdot 3 \cdot 13)(7 \cdot 17 \ undo {mm})\\
&amp;= (3 \cdot 7 \cdot 3 \cdot 17)\cdot (13)\\
&amp;= 3^2 \cdot 7 \cdot 13 \cdot 17
\end{align\*}There are 4 total factors. Given this prime factorization, the prime factors are $3,13,7,17$.
Do you see how this became 4?
The numbers $7,13,17$ and $3$ are 4 different prime factors.
\end{document} 

